.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1038 PM PDT Fri Oct 23 2026
(Today and tonight)

Gusty offshore flow across the North Bay Interior Mountains is
ongoing tonight, with the windy and dry conditions dropping MinRH
values in the 20s for some locations. Fire weather concerns would be
higher for this area had we not received the recent wetting
rainfall. The offshore flow regime should mostly limit fog
development early this morning but still can`t be ruled out entirely
in some of the river valleys in Sonoma County.

Temperatures today will be around 5 degrees warmer than Friday and
near normal for this time of year. The gusty winds across the North
Bay will begin to ease by late this morning, with a beautiful day in
store area wide across our CWA as upper level ridging builds
overhead.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1038 PM PDT Fri Oct 23 2026
(Sunday through next Friday)

Sunday will almost be a carbon copy of today for the North Bay and
Bay Area, with the Central Coast seeing temperatures warm another
few degrees. Confidence and consensus in the synoptic pattern is
poor after Monday. An upper trough shifts south to our latitude on
Monday bringing a return of onshore flow, however the overall
progressive pattern is struggling with the depth of the trough and
how another system well offshore interacts with the flattening
trough as it moves into the Great Basin. Despite the uncertainty in
the overall pattern, any troughing or ridging will continue to be
progressive and low amplitude, with no large temperature fluctuations
or precipitation chances until the end of next week when the NBM
seems to pick up on what the current ECMWF deterministic has
depicted for next Friday into the weekend, troughing with increasing
rain chances.

